    <div class="bg-secondary/40 border-l-4 border-primary rounded-r-lg px-5 py-4 mb-6">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-1">Direct Answer</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">No keyboard tray is engineered for tall bodies — the tall-specific part is the desk height that surrounds it. A 6'4" user sets a standing surface at 43"–45" and a 6'6" user at 45"–47", and a flat typing surface at that height drives the wrist into extension. Standard keyboards make it worse, adding roughly 5°–11° of positive tilt through their rear feet. A tray offering 10°–20° of negative tilt cancels both and returns the wrist to the neutral 0°–15° band. Treat it as a companion to a raised desk or converter, not a standalone tall product.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-lg px-5 py-4 mb-8">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-1">Honest framing up front</p>
      <p class="text-sm">Keyboard trays are not sold in tall versions and none of the products below were designed around user height. This page exists because raising a desk or converter to a tall user's correct height creates a wrist problem that shorter users rarely encounter. The tray solves that downstream problem. If you have not yet solved the desk height itself, start with <a href="/standing-desk-converter-tall-people/" class="link-internal">standing desk converters for tall people →</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <Disclosure />

    <div class="prose-tca">

      <p>I'm Jackson Christopher — 6'4", Mechanical Engineering senior at UC Berkeley. The Steelcase Gesture is the only product I have personally used and can speak to first-hand. Everything below is spec analysis and wrist-joint geometry, drawn from published manufacturer dimensions and reported experience from tall users in r/ergonomics and r/officechair.</p>

      <p>I want to be direct about what this page is, because a lot of tall-keyword content oversells the connection. There is no such thing as a tall person's keyboard tray. What exists is a predictable chain: tall body, higher correct desk height, raised typing surface, extended wrist. The tray is the link that breaks the chain.</p>

      <section>
        <h2>Why Raising a Desk to Tall Height Creates a Wrist Problem</h2>

        <p>Start with the wrist joint itself. Neutral means the back of the hand is roughly in line with the forearm — zero degrees of extension. Ergonomics guidance broadly targets keeping the wrist within about 0°–15° of extension during typing, because pressure inside the carpal tunnel rises steeply as extension increases beyond that band. Sustained extension while typing is one of the better-documented contributors to wrist and forearm discomfort in desk work.</p>

        <p>Now stack up what a tall user actually does to a workstation:</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Raise the surface.</strong> Standing elbow height at 6'4" is approximately 45", and at 6'6" approximately 47". The typing surface goes to 43"–45" or 45"–47" respectively.</li>
          <li><strong>The surface is flat.</strong> Converter keyboard decks and desktops are horizontal, with no tilt adjustment.</li>
          <li><strong>The keyboard adds positive tilt.</strong> Nearly every keyboard has flip-out rear feet that raise the back edge, creating roughly 5°–11° of upward slope toward the user.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>The result is a surface that already slopes the wrong way, and any error toward setting it too high compounds the extension. If the surface ends up even an inch or two above elbow height — easy to do when a converter's steps are 2" apart — the forearm angles downward to reach it while the hand must angle back up to stay on the keys. That difference is absorbed entirely by the wrist.</p>

        <h3>The moment-arm view</h3>

        <p>An engineering way to see it: the finger flexor tendons run through the carpal tunnel like cables through a conduit. Extending the wrist bends the conduit. A bent path means the tendons press against the tunnel walls and against the median nerve, and it also increases the tension the muscles must generate for the same keystroke force because the effective moment arm changes. Higher tendon load through a narrower opening, repeated tens of thousands of times a day. That is the mechanism, and it is geometry, not effort.</p>

        <p>Negative tilt reverses step two. Dropping the far edge of the keyboard 10°–20° below the near edge means the fingers descend to the keys rather than reaching up to them, and the wrist stays flat or slightly flexed. It is the same correction that keyboard trays have offered for decades — it is just that tall users hit the triggering condition, an above-elbow typing surface, far more often.</p>

        <p class="bg-secondary/30 border-l-2 border-primary/40 pl-4 py-1 text-sm italic">Negative-tilt keyboard trays correct wrist extension caused by raised typing surfaces, not by user height. Standing elbow height is approximately 45" at 6'4" and approximately 47" at 6'6", so a tall user's typing surface sits 14"–18" above a 29" desktop where flat decks and the 5°–11° positive tilt of standard keyboard feet drive the wrist out of the neutral 0°–15° extension band. Trays offering 10°–20° of negative tilt reverse both effects.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Seated Versus Standing: Two Different Tray Jobs</h2>

        <p>The correct tray height is not one number, and tall users tend to conflate the two cases.</p>

        <h3>Seated at a standard desk</h3>

        <p>This surprises people who assume tall users always need things higher. Seated elbow height at 6'4" is roughly 27"–29" with the chair set correctly — feet flat, thighs level. A standard desk is 29"–30". So seated, the desktop is at or slightly <em>above</em> a tall user's elbow, and an under-desk tray that drops the keyboard 1"–3" is genuinely the right move.</p>

        <p>The catch is legroom, and it is a real constraint for long femurs. This is the same clearance problem that governs <a href="/knee-pain-seat-depth/" class="link-internal">seat depth and knee position →</a>, viewed from above instead of behind.</p>

        <h3>Standing at a raised desk or converter</h3>

        <p>Here the tray needs to go up, not down. A sit/stand tray that mounts under the desktop but rises above it covers this case: the keyboard sits below the desktop when seated and above it when standing, without moving the mount. The Fellowes Professional Series tray is the example in this comparison, with a published range extending up to 6.25" above the desktop.</p>

