        <h3>Uncaged Ergonomics KT2-b — Best Budget Negative-Tilt Tray</h3>

        <p><strong>Type:</strong> under-desk, adjustable height and angle &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Tilt:</strong> adjustable including negative &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Approx. price:</strong> $70–$90</p>

        <p>The KT2-b is an under-desk tray with independent height and angle adjustment, and negative tilt is part of its published capability. At $70–$90 it is the cheapest way to test whether wrist extension is what has been bothering you, which is the right first experiment before spending three times as much.</p>

        <p>Confidence here is high on the mechanism and the fit for seated work. What I will not do is quote an exact tilt figure in degrees, because the listing does not publish one consistently — treat "adjustable negative tilt" as the claim and verify the achievable angle when it arrives. That distinction matters more than it sounds: a tray that reaches 5° of negative tilt will not cancel a keyboard's own 8° of positive slope, while one reaching 15° will.</p>

        <p>For a tall user the relevant check before ordering is vertical clearance. Measure floor-to-underside of your desk, subtract the tray's mounting depth, and confirm the result still clears your knees at roughly 24"–26" for a 6'4" frame.</p>

