        <p>The UPLIFT Large publishes a 20° negative to 10° positive tilt range, which is the specification that actually matters here. Twenty degrees of negative tilt is enough to cancel a keyboard's built-in 5°–11° positive slope and still leave 9°–15° of genuine downward angle. That is comfortably inside the range where the wrist returns to neutral.</p>

        <p>The large surface is the second reason to consider it. Tall users have longer forearms and wider natural elbow spacing, and a narrow tray forces the arms inward toward the body — the same mechanism that makes narrow armrests uncomfortable and contributes to <a href="/shoulder-pain-tall-people/" class="link-internal">shoulder tension in tall desk users →</a>. A tray sized for a full-width keyboard plus mouse keeps both hands in front of the shoulders rather than crowded together.</p>

