      <section>
        <h2>How to Set Tray Height and Tilt</h2>

        <h3>Step 1: Measure elbow height in the posture you are fixing</h3>
        <p>Seated: feet flat, thighs level, forearms at 90 degrees, measure floor to underside of elbow. Standing: same, upright in work shoes. At 6'4" expect roughly 27"–29" seated and roughly 45" standing. Fix one posture at a time.</p>

        <h3>Step 2: Put the home row at or just below elbow height</h3>
        <p>Not the tray surface — the middle row of keys. Up to 2" below the elbow is fine and often preferable. Above elbow height is the condition that creates the whole problem.</p>

        <h3>Step 3: Retract the keyboard feet, then add negative tilt</h3>
        <p>Fold the rear feet down first. That alone removes 5°–11° of positive slope, and a surprising number of people never do it. Then tilt the tray so the far edge drops until the back of your hand lines up with your forearm. Most users settle between 10° and 20°.</p>

        <h3>Step 4: Check knee clearance before you commit</h3>
        <p>Measure floor to the underside of the tray at its lowest position. Compare against seated knee height, roughly 24"–26" at 6'4". If the track contacts your thighs, the tray is the wrong mount type for your desk regardless of how good its tilt range is.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>When a Tray Is the Wrong Purchase</h2>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Your desk is too low, not too high.</strong> If you are hunching down to a 28" desk seated, the answer is raising the desk, not lowering the keyboard further. See <a href="/standing-desk-height-tall-people/" class="link-internal">standing desk height for tall people →</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Legroom is already tight.</strong> A tray and track eat 1.5"–3" of under-desk clearance. If your thighs already contact the desk underside, a tray makes an existing problem worse.</li>
          <li><strong>The real issue is the chair.</strong> Wrist symptoms sometimes trace back to armrests that cannot reach elbow height, leaving the forearms unsupported and the wrists compensating. Rule that out first with <a href="/correct-chair-dimensions/" class="link-internal">the chair dimensions guide →</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>You have a converter with a dedicated tilting tray.</strong> Some converters already separate the keyboard deck and allow tilt. Check what you own before buying a second solution to the same problem.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Do tall people need a special keyboard tray?</h3>
        <p>No. No tray is engineered around body height, and none of the three above claims to be. The tall-specific factor is the raised typing surface — 43"–45" standing at 6'4", 45"–47" at 6'6" — which creates the wrist extension a negative-tilt tray corrects. Buy it as a companion to a raised desk or converter.</p>

        <h3>What is a negative-tilt keyboard tray and why does it matter?</h3>
        <p>Negative tilt slopes the keyboard away from you, far edge lower than near edge. Standard keyboards do the opposite, adding roughly 5°–11° of positive tilt through their rear feet, which pushes the wrist into extension. Neutral is about 0°–15° of extension, and carpal tunnel pressure climbs steeply past that. A 10°–20° negative tilt cancels the keyboard's slope and restores the neutral angle.</p>

        <h3>How high should a keyboard tray sit for a tall person?</h3>
        <p>At or up to 2" below elbow height in the posture you are using. At 6'4", that is roughly 27"–29" seated — usually 1"–3" below a standard desktop — and roughly 43"–45" standing. Set the home row to that height, then adjust tilt.</p>

        <h3>Can a keyboard tray be used with a standing desk converter?</h3>
        <p>Yes, and it is frequently necessary because many converters use a flat, non-tilting keyboard deck. A sit/stand tray such as the Fellowes Professional Series, with up to 6.25" of above-desktop travel, tackles the same problem from the desk side. Check mounting clearance first, since converters occupy 20"–24" of desk depth and can block the track.</p>

        <h3>Does an under-desk keyboard tray reduce legroom for tall users?</h3>
        <p>Yes — roughly 1.5"–3" of vertical clearance for the tray and its track. A 6'4" user seated correctly has about 24"–26" of knee clearance under a 29"–30" desk, so that loss can produce thigh contact. Measure floor-to-desk-underside and subtract the mounting depth before ordering.</p>

        <h3>Will a negative-tilt tray help if my wrists hurt at a normal seated desk?</h3>
        <p>Often, yes, and the fix may be free. Retracting the keyboard's rear feet removes 5°–11° of positive tilt on its own. If that helps but not enough, a tray adds the remaining 10°–20° of correction and drops the surface 1"–3" below the desktop, which is where a tall user's seated elbow already sits.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
        <p>A keyboard tray is not a tall-person product, and any page telling you otherwise is stretching. It is the correction for a condition tall users create when they finally set their desk to the right height: a typing surface at or above elbow level, on a flat deck, under a keyboard that already tilts the wrong way.</p>
        <p>Filter on published negative-tilt range first — the UPLIFT's 20° is the clearest number in the group — then on height behavior, then on whether it physically fits under your desk without stealing knee room. Solve desk height first; add the tray second.</p>
      </section>

